  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 1202 AM CST Thu Nov 12 2026\n\nSaturday night through Wednesday:\n\nAcross the CONUS a ridge will set up across the Northern Plains\nand Great Lakes Region while a cutoff low traverses through the\nsouthern Plains and into the Mid Atlantic. With the surface high\npressure from Canada settling in across the Plains/Great Lakes\nFriday night into Saturday, dry and cold weather is expected. As\nthe colder air mass begins to move in, overnight lows are\nexpected to fall into the low to mid 20s for inland areas and\nmid 20s to low 30s along the lakeshore. Sunny skies with highs\nin the mid 40s to low 50s are expected through the weekend and\ninto early next week. \n\nRidging aloft and the sfc high will linger across the central\nCONUS through mid week. Both features will slowly be moving east\nwith time, but it will be very gradual. The sfc high should be\neast of Wisconsin by midday Monday, which will bring a return \nto southwesterly flow and some slightly warmer air. This will \nallow temperatures to start recovering through the week back \ninto the mid 50s with model guidance trying to hint temperatures\ncontinuing to warm into Friday. Before we can get to the end of\nthe week, there is growing consensus on a low pressure system \ndeveloping in the rocky mountain west and moving northeast \ntoward Lake Superior Thursday. This low will be accompanied by \nan upper level trough and the subsequent cold front that will \nmove across Wisconsin. The passage of the cold front could bring\nrain back to the area. There is still uncertainty in the timing\nand track of the upper level trough. Which will impact the \ndevelopment of the sfc low in the lee of the rockies and its \npath. So POPs remain fairly low around 30%. \n\nPatterson",
